\DOC RENAME_DFG_TAC

\TYPE {RENAME_DFG_TAC : term -> term -> term -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Renames three designated variables of the goal to three supplied new names,
folds the three compound values that now occur in the assumptions and
conclusion back to the old names, and adds three equations linking them.

\DESCRIBE
The arguments {d0 : term}, {f0 : term}, {g0 : term} are the three new variable
names (of integer type) replacing the goal's three existing variables, in that
order: {d0} for the first renamed variable, {f0} for the second, {g0} for the
third. Writing {x1,x2,x3} for the old variables, every occurrence of them in
the assumptions and in the conclusion becomes the corresponding argument term;
at the same time three specific compound expressions built from the renamed
variables, one for each of the three roles, are folded back to {x1,x2,x3}
wherever they occur in the assumptions, which makes several assumptions much
shorter. Three assumptions are appended, each equating one of those compound
expressions with the corresponding old variable name. Assumptions mentioning
none of the affected terms are unchanged in content, although the whole
assumption list is rebuilt, so all touched assumptions are removed and
re-added.
{
             A ?- t
   ===================================================  RENAME_DFG_TAC d0 f0 g0
   A[x1,x2,x3 := d0,f0,g0], with the three defining
   expressions folded back to x1,x2,x3, plus the three
   equations ?- t[x1,x2,x3 := d0,f0,g0]
}

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, always on
goals of the same shape: a machine-state {eventually} conclusion with a large
assumption list of register-read and bound facts.

\ENDDOC
